.SH AI AGENT
Kepler's Horizon supports single-player mode through an autonomous AI
opponent called the \fBAutonomy Agency\fR.
The AI replaces one human player and executes commands through the same
game engine, respecting all rules and constraints.
It issues the same commands a human player would type.
.PP
The AI thread runs independently of HTTP request handling.
When a game ends (via \fBquit\fR, victory, or elimination), the AI's
game context is zeroed\(emit is \fBnever killed\fR.
The thread stays alive and naturally ceases operating when it detects
\fBm_game_id = 0\fR on its next gather cycle.
.SS "Architecture: Mealy State Machine"
The AI uses a three-phase decision cycle:
.IP "1. GATHER (C++)" 4
Populate an \fBAASlate\fR data structure from the database and
StateMachine.  The \fBSlate\fR is the \fISlate of State\fR which means
a scratch board of \fImetrics\fR that are gleaned from sources within
the game, during steps conducted by the State Machine of the AI-Agency.
The slate contains the complete observable game: fleet positions,
combat theaters, economic data, territory control, BFS distance
matrix, hex adjacency, warpline topology, codex entries, market
prices, and cross-turn metrics.
.IP "2. CALCULATE (Lisp)" 4
Marshal the slate to Common Lisp via ECL (Embeddable Common Lisp),
call the strategy function \fB(aa-calculate slate)\fR, and unmarshal
the result: a list of command strings and optional metric updates.
.IP "3. RENDER (C++)" 4
Inject the resulting command string into the game engine's command
queue via \fBAICommandInjector\fR, exactly as if a human typed it.
Block until the TaskRunner completes the command.  The game engine is
designed to handle two-player or single-player modes.  ALL commands
are funneled through \f(CWyyparse()\fR -- even the AI-Agency commands --
this is mostly to avoid duplicative work in the server-side and also
make use of the return-codes (success or failure) that may be obtained
by the commands themselves. \f(CWTaskRunner\fR as a thread simply
invokes commands regardless of their initator.  User-player or AI-Agency.
But, the AI-Agency, when enabled in single-player mode pays attention
to the results of the Task Runner and always modifies the Slate
and therefore may modulate the \fInext command\fR based on outcomes
from the prior AI-Agency invoked commands.   The AI-Agency is adaptive.
.PP
The cycle repeats (gather fresh state, recalculate, render) until the
AI issues a terminal command (\fBNEXT\fR or \fBDONE\fR) to advance the
turn.
This closed-loop design means every decision is based on current game
state, not stale assumptions.
.PP
A safety guard at the top of \fBgather()\fR checks \fBm_game_id <= 0\fR
and sets \fBgame_over = true\fR, causing the Mealy loop to exit
gracefully without querying the database.
.SS "Strategy DSL Modules"
The AI strategy is implemented across eight Common Lisp modules,
loaded in dependency order:
.TP
.B aa-util.lisp
Accessor functions for reading the data slate.
Ship property lookups, list filters, and helper predicates.
.TP
.B aa-strategy.lisp
High-level strategic ontology (A1\-A11).
Computes posture (aggressive/defensive/balanced), theater coordination,
and inter-phase directives.
Uses plist flow: \fBcompute-posture\fR \[->] theater coordination \[->]
build/movement directives.
.TP
.B aa-goals.lisp
Goal evaluation engine using model-predictive control.
Evaluates objectives in priority order and finds the highest-priority
unsatisfied goal.
.TP
.B aa-economic.lisp
Maps economic goals to concrete commands: survey, extract, buy,
fabricate, sell, outfit, salvage.
.TP
.B aa-build.lisp
Ship design selection, construction, repair, and deployment decisions.
Selects from predefined ship templates based on game state.
.TP
.B aa-movement.lisp
Movement routing with BFS pathfinding.
Prioritizes defending threatened bases and pushing ships toward
undefended enemy bases for VP capture.
Spreads ships across different enemy bases to maximize VP coverage.
.TP
.B aa-combat.lisp
Multi-theater triage, CRT-aware power allocation, target selection
(focus fire on lowest-HP enemy), damage assignment
(sacrifice tubes first, then beams, screens, PD last).
.TP
.B aa-core.lisp
Main entry point and phase dispatcher.
Routes decisions by turn phase (build, movement, combat, pick/drop,
end turn).
.SS "Build Strategy"
The AI selects from predefined ship templates based on game state:
.TS
tab(|);
l l l.
Template|Attributes|Role
_
Brawler|PD=6 B=4 S=3|Main battle ship; built first
Interceptor|PD=5 B=2 S=2|Fast pursuit; spreads to bases
Missile Boat|PD=4 S=1 T=2 M=6|Alpha strike; counters heavy screens
Fortress|PD=8 B=6 S=5|SystemShip for base defense
.TE
.PP
Template selection considers enemy fleet composition, tech level timing,
and fleet diversity.
The AI caps its fleet at 5 WarpShips.
.SS "Combat Strategy"
The AI assesses all active combat theaters simultaneously:
.IP \(bu 2
Force ratio (our PD / their PD) determines fight/hold/retreat
.IP \(bu 2
VP hexes (enemy bases) are defended more aggressively
.IP \(bu 2
All ships in one hex focus fire on the same target (lowest total HP)
.IP \(bu 2
Drive differential is clamped to avoid +5 automatic miss
.IP \(bu 2
Damage assignment: sacrifice tubes first, then beams, screens, PD last
.SS "Economic Strategy"
The AI evaluates economic objectives in priority order:
.IP 1. 3
Resupply missiles at base (if stock below 50%)
.IP 2. 3
Fabricate missiles (if materials available at shipyard)
.IP 3. 3
Acquire resources needed for fabrication (extract or buy)
.IP 4. 3
Survey unknown systems for resource yields
.IP 5. 3
Outfit ships with drones (enables salvage/extraction)
.IP 6. 3
Salvage wreckage at anomaly sites
.IP 7. 3
Sell excess cargo at trade hubs
.PP
Cargo is never sold while it serves an active goal.
.SS "Cross-Turn Memory"
The AI persists deduced metrics to the \fBaa_metrics\fR database table
via \fB(make-metric name value)\fR pseudo-commands emitted by Lisp.
These metrics survive across decision cycles and are loaded back into
the slate each gather.
.\" ===================================================================
.SH SYSTEMSHIP PICKUP AND DROP
.SS "During Movement Phase"
.IP \(bu 2
Costs 1 PD per SystemShip picked up or dropped
.IP \(bu 2
Can be performed at any star hex during movement
.SS "During Combat"
.IP \(bu 2
Requires: Drive=0, Screen=0, Dodge or Escape tactic
.IP \(bu 2
Only 1 SystemShip per combat round
.IP \(bu 2
May fire Beam (but not Missiles) while picking up/dropping
.IP \(bu 2
Dropped SystemShips cannot fire that round
.IP \(bu 2
Picked up SystemShips cannot fire but may power Screens
.SS "After Combat (Phase 5)"
.IP \(bu 2
Free rearrangement of SystemShips at star hexes
.IP \(bu 2
No PD cost
.IP \(bu 2
Active player only
.\" ===================================================================
.SH BUILD POINT COSTS
.TS
tab(|);
l l.
Attribute|Cost
_
Power/Drive (PD)|1 BP per unit
Warp Generator (WG)|5 BP (WarpShips only, automatic)
Beam (B)|1 BP per unit
Screen (S)|1 BP per unit
Tube (T)|1 BP per tube
Missiles (M)|1 BP per 3 missiles
