.SH "Machine Learning: AI Agent Feedback Loop & Latent Learning"

.PP
Design issues about closing the feedback loop between command execution and AI agent decision-making,
and a longer-term vision for build-time machine learning driven by game diaries.

.SH "1. The Problem: Open-Loop Command Execution"

.PP
The AI agent's MPC (model-predictive control) loop is currently open-loop at the RENDER step.
The cycle is:

.PP
.nf
\f(CWGATHER -> CALCULATE -> RENDER -> (assume success) -> GATHER\fR
.fi

.PP
A human player closes this loop naturally -- they see "Invalid move" on screen and adjust.
The AI agent has no equivalent feedback channel.

.PP
When \f(CWinvoke()\fR returns false on a command, the TaskRunner knows the command failed and logs the error.
But \f(CWnotify_task_complete()\fR is a bare signal -- it carries no payload.
The agent wakes up from \f(CWwait_for_task_completion()\fR knowing only "the task finished," not whether it
succeeded or failed, or why.

.PP
This causes blind retry loops: the agent re-issues the same failing command because it never learns it failed.
The game state hasn't changed, so \f(CWCALCULATE\fR derives the same command from the same inputs.

.SH "2. The Gap Is Narrow"

.PP
The information already exists at the right place. In \f(CWtaskrunner.cpp\fR, after executing an AI task:

.PP
.RS
.nf
.ft CW
int result = internal_command_handler_body(item->ai_command, errmsg);
.ft R
.fi
.RE

.PP
The TaskRunner has both \f(CWresult\fR (success/failure) and \f(CWerrmsg\fR (the reason).
It logs them, then calls \f(CWnotify_task_complete()\fR which discards both.
The plumbing for the blocking handshake between TaskRunner and AI agent thread already exists via condition
variable -- it just signals a bool when it could signal a result structure.

.SH "3. Proposed Feedback Mechanism"

.SS "Task Meta-Data"

.PP
Each Task carries meta-data that is populated by the grammar rule involved in the \f(CWinvoke()\fR call.
This is not just a bool -- it includes contextual information about \fIwhy\fR the command failed:

.IP "\(bu" 2
The command that was attempted
.IP "\(bu" 2
Success or failure
.IP "\(bu" 2
Structured failure reason (not a human-readable string)

.SS "Notification With Payload"

.PP
When \f(CWnotify_task_complete()\fR fires, the meta-data blob is conveyed to the AI agent.
The agent only cares about failures -- successful commands need no special handling since the world state
changes and the next \f(CWGATHER\fR reflects reality.

.SS "Persistence in the Slate"

.PP
Failed-command meta-data persists in the agent's awareness and becomes part of the slate during the next
\f(CWGATHER\fR step. The Lisp code can then see: "my last MOVE w6 2223 failed because X" and choose differently.

.SH "4. Failure Reasons as Ontology Facts"

.PP
Rather than carrying free-form error strings that Lisp would have to parse, failure reasons should be closed
under the ontology dictionary. Every failure cause maps 1:1 to an ontology fact:

.IP "\(bu" 2
"insufficient credits" -> \f(CW:precondition-violated :credits-below-threshold\fR
.IP "\(bu" 2
"hex contested" -> \f(CW:precondition-violated :hex-in-combat\fR
.IP "\(bu" 2
"not your turn" -> \f(CW:precondition-violated :phase-mismatch\fR

.PP
This means the Lisp code reasons about failures the same way it reasons about everything else -- through the
strategy plist, using the same subsystem functions. Failures become just another structured input to
\f(CWCALCULATE\fR, not a special side channel.

.PP
The closed-set property guarantees the Lisp code can handle every failure because the ontology is finite and
known at compile time.

.SH "5. Failure Lifetime: When to Forget"

.SS "Time-Based Expiry Is Wrong"

.PP
A fixed TTL (e.g., "forget after 3 rounds") is either too long -- the agent avoids a now-valid command for rounds
after the blocker cleared -- or too short -- the agent retries a still-invalid command because the timer expired.

.SS "State-Change Invalidation Is Ideal But Hard"

.PP
The theoretically correct approach: a failure stays relevant until the slate shows the precondition that caused it
has changed. But this requires mapping each failure reason to the specific state that caused it, which is a complex,
fragile mapping.

.SS "Phase-Scoped Lifetime Is Pragmatic"

.PP
Clear the failure list at each phase transition. Within a single phase, game state only changes through the agent's
own actions (and the other player's during combat). A command that fails in BUILD phase round 5 won't succeed later in
BUILD phase round 5 -- the preconditions are stable within a phase.

.PP
Exception: combat, where state changes within a phase as orders resolve, damage is assigned, and ships escape.
Combat may need round-scoped or stage-scoped clearing.

.SH "6. Latent Machine Learning: The Diary Concept"

.SS "Two Timescales"

.PP
Separate the learning feedback loop into two timescales:

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBRuntime (within a game):\fR Static Lisp, fixed ontology, metrics memory. No self-modification.
Deterministic and debuggable.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBBuild-time (between releases):\fR Ingest accumulated game diaries, produce updated Lisp parameters.
The agent evolves, but only through a controlled, auditable build step.

.SS "The Diary"

.PP
Extend the existing \f(CWaa_metrics\fR persistence to capture richer game-over summaries: what round the game ended,
who won, fleet composition at key moments, which strategies were attempted and when. Structured data, not prose.

.SS "Translation Approaches (Build-Time Ingestion)"

.PP
Three approaches, ordered by complexity:

.PP
\fBApproach 1: Parameter Tables (Recommended Starting Point)\fR

.PP
The diary feeds a statistical pass that produces a config file -- thresholds, weights, timing windows.
Example: "average earliest viable missile build = round 5.2 across 47 games" becomes a \f(CWdefparameter\fR value.
No new Lisp code is generated. The build step is data aggregation. Lisp code has \f(CWdefparameter\fR declarations
that get values from a generated config.

.PP
Simplest, safest, and likely captures 80% of the benefit.

.PP
\fBApproach 2: Rule Selection\fR

.PP
Write multiple strategy variants by hand -- aggressive early-build, conservative turtle, missile-rush, etc.
Diary analysis determines which variants performed best against different opponent profiles.
The build step produces a selection table. No generated Lisp, just generated configuration selecting among
pre-written variants.

.PP
\fBApproach 3: Lisp Code Generation\fR

.PP
An external tool reads the diary, identifies patterns, and emits new Lisp functions or modifies existing ones.
Requires a validation step -- generated Lisp must type-check against the ontology, return well-formed plists,
and not introduce infinite loops. This is a research project more than an engineering task.

.SS "Build System Integration"

.PP
For approaches 1 and 2: a pre-build script reads the diary database, runs the analysis, writes a \f(CW.lisp\fR file of
\f(CWdefparameter\fR declarations or a strategy-selection table, and the normal build includes that file.
No special tooling beyond a script that queries the metrics database and emits s-expressions.

.SS "On Self-Modifying Lisp"

.PP
The power of the current architecture is that Lisp functions are pure: slate in, decisions out.
They carry no hidden state, don't mutate themselves, don't evolve at runtime.
That makes them debuggable, predictable, and auditable.
Self-modifying code at runtime sacrifices all three properties.
The diary/build-time approach preserves them by restricting evolution to a controlled, versioned build step.

